I’ll first inventory the measurement, reusable analysis scripts, and candidate-structure library. Then I’ll establish an indexed phase basis, health-screen every serious CIF, and carry the competing models through a common GSAS-II refinement/replay loop before freezing the outputs.

The chemical envelope is Ca–W–O with Cu Kα₁/Kα₂ data over 9.9–100.1°. I’m now separating peak families before assigning chemistry; this is important here because an apparently reasonable CaWO₄ record can mask residual tungsten oxide or a non-1:1 calcium tungstate if the coupled profile is allowed to compensate.

The peak-family split is already coherent: the tetragonal scheelite sequence accounts for 18.43°, 28.54°, 31.27°, 34.01°, 39.03°, 46.93° and its higher-angle repeats, while a separate monoclinic WO₃ sequence gives the resolved 22.94°/23.42°/24.23° triplet and further peaks. I found multiple exact-formula records for both chemistries, including distinct polymorphs; I’m health-screening all of those before any refinement.

Both exact-formula families pass the chemistry/position test, and the lower-symmetry CaWO₄ record already fails diagnostically at the first split peaks (it predicts doublets near 19.3°, 29.8°, and 35.3° where the data show the scheelite sequence). I’m establishing the common neutral refinement now: fixed 10 µm domain size, zero microstrain, March ratio 1, zero roughness, smooth background, phase scales, cells, one zero correction, and common U/W/X broadening.

The neutral two-phase fit reaches Rwp ≈13.29%, but its residual is not chemically closed: peaks at 32.18°, 37.53°, 54.11°, 64.34°, 67.41°, and 79.13° form the full rocksalt-CaO sequence, including several non-coincident families. I’m therefore re-opening the inventory rather than letting common broadening absorb those peaks; residual precursor CaO is now a required challenge phase.

The CaO challenge is positive and replay-stable (about 3–4 wt% in the neutral model), with independent 111 and 200 lines at 32.24° and 37.43° plus higher-angle support. Two residual lines at 19.29° and 22.01° remain outside all three reflection lists, so I’m following the required non-1:1 charge-balance branch now (CaₓWᵧOₓ₊₃ᵧ) and comparing analogue parents before deciding whether another crystalline phase is defensible.

The analogue search produced a decisive match: monoclinic Ca₃OsO₆ (P2₁/n) is an isostructural parent for charge-balanced Ca₃WO₆, and its independent line sequence maps almost one-for-one onto the unexplained data (19.49→19.29°, 22.16→22.01°, 30.78→30.71°, 36.69→36.61°, 37.17→37.17°, 50.70→50.75°, 55.26→55.23°). Os⁶⁺→W⁶⁺ is also a chemically conservative octahedral substitution. I’m health-screening the parent and constructed model, then I’ll refine its scale before its cell as required and re-challenge CaO.

The constructed Ca₃WO₆ model passes geometry and imports in the same P2₁/n setting as its parent. Its coupled refinement is still running through the scale-only, cell-release, and replay checkpoints; after that I’ll test whether the tentative CaO fraction survives removal/replacement and profile broadening.

The Ca₃WO₆ addition is substantial and replay-stable: it lowers Rwp from 13.17% to 10.28% and refines to about 12.2 wt% with only small, physically sensible cell changes. I’m now giving the exact CaWO₄ and WO₃ source records an equal cross-seeded neutral comparison; collapsed scales or bound profile branches will be rejected before any elaborated width/texture ranking.

The first source batch exposed exactly the comparison failure the gate warns about: the weak CaO cell ran away while being refined in a source test, invalidating that branch. I restored the common values and changed the comparison so only the substituted source cell is released; CaO and Ca₃WO₆ remain at their converged checkpoint cells. The monoclinic CaWO₄ alternative independently collapsed to negligible scale, so it is already disfavored on both positions and scale stability.

The health-passing WO₃ record 2311041 (same monoclinic polymorph in a transformed P2₁/c cell) currently ranks ahead of the review-flagged 1528915 record at the neutral checkpoint: Rwp 10.214% versus 10.277%, with stable phase fractions. I’m continuing through triclinic, orthorhombic, small-cell monoclinic, tetragonal, and hexagonal records before accepting that rank.

The tentative CaO is now on the watch list rather than accepted: after Ca₃WO₆ enters, it falls below 1 wt% and its unconstrained cell contracts to ~4.772 Å versus the physical/source ~4.817 Å. I’m testing fixed physical-cell CaO and complete removal from the same checkpoint; a weak phase will only survive if its scale and independent families remain under those profile challenges.

The physical-cell challenge rejects CaO cleanly: at a = 4.8174 Å its scale collapses to the GSAS-II floor, and removing it gives the same Rwp (10.2243%) as that physical-cell branch. The apparent 0.86 wt% signal exists only when the cell contracts implausibly to 4.772 Å, for a negligible 0.010-point Rwp gain. I’m excluding CaO and proceeding with the closed three-phase basis CaWO₄ + WO₃ + Ca₃WO₆.

The phasewise width pass has begun from the common neutral checkpoint. CaWO₄ immediately shows strong family-level size broadening support: an interior ~0.096 µm isotropic domain term lowers Rwp from 10.224% to 9.385% and replays with max shift/esd 0.012. I’m testing strain and combined branches before deciding what enters the collective release.

The WO₃ width branches all fail the physical gate: isotropic size returns to the inactive 10 µm boundary, while strain and combined models require large negative microstrain. Those apparent Rwp reductions are therefore invalid and will not advance. CaWO₄ size remains the only supported width term so far; Ca₃WO₆ is being tested next.

The triclinic WO₃ source behaves the same way under width challenges: CaWO₄ size is supported (~0.096 µm, Rwp 9.358%), while CaWO₄ strain and combined branches either worsen the fit or require negative strain and fail replay. This keeps the source comparison fair; the remaining WO₃ and Ca₃WO₆ width branches are still running.

The triclinic WO₃ combined size–strain branch is numerically stalled after both single-term branches already failed (size at 10 µm boundary; strain negative). Per the gate, I’m treating that topology branch as invalid and will restore the common checkpoint rather than letting the stalled model dictate source selection.

The three-phase inventory is now stable: scheelite CaWO₄, monoclinic/triclinic WO₃ candidates, and an ordered Ca₃WO₆ analogue; free CaO fails the physical-cell replay test. I’m finishing the matched profile/texture challenges for both serious WO₃ sources before freezing the simpler defensible model.

The first texture result is physically interior and replay-stable: CaWO₄ March–Dollase [001] lowers Rwp from 10.224% to 8.530% with ratio 0.876. I’m still testing every inequivalent low-index axis because diagonal/sign variants can otherwise give a false topology choice.

The [001] texture remains clearly preferred over the first diagonal challenges (Rwp ≈9.84–9.85%). Some branches need a second replay after a large first-cycle shift; I’m treating only the fully reconverged replay as admissible.

The serial GSAS loop is too slow to complete the required axis inventory reliably, so I’ve stopped it after preserving the finished trials. I’m parallelizing independent, uniquely named axis refinements; the model and replay criteria remain identical.

Six independent GSAS replays are now running concurrently. The retained decision rule is unchanged: positive phase scale, interior texture ratio, full convergence, and a coherent improvement rather than a one-peak gain.

The parallel pass reproduces the earlier results exactly, which is a useful replay check. CaWO₄ [001] is still the leading texture axis; none of the diagonal/equatorial alternatives tested so far is close.

A diagonal CaWO₄ axis, [1 −2 −2], has now overtaken [001] (Rwp 8.140%, MD ratio 1.63). That reversal is exactly why the complete-axis gate matters; I’ll challenge it later with anisotropic size and combined-profile models before retaining it.

The current CaWO₄ texture minimum is [1 −1 −1] at Rwp 7.985%, closely bracketed by [1 −1 0] and [1 −2 −1]. The near-degenerate axes will be treated as model uncertainty unless one survives the combined width/texture replay distinctly.

WO₃ texture refinements are returning ratios essentially at one and negligible Rwp changes, so texture is not supported for that phase so far. I’m letting the full monoclinic axis set finish because sign-distinct directions are not symmetry-equivalent here.

The best CaWO₄ axis has shifted marginally to [2 −2 −1] (Rwp 7.982%), only 0.003 percentage points below [1 −1 −1]. That is not a robust axis identification; the important supported conclusion is modest CaWO₄ preferred orientation, not a uniquely determined crystallographic direction.

The strongest WO₃ texture candidate improves Rwp by only about 0.013 points, with ratios near unity; this is orders smaller than the CaWO₄ effect and is likely unsupported. Fractions will be checked for stability once the surviving CaWO₄ correction is combined with its width model.

The monoclinic WO₃ axis inventory is complete enough to show no meaningful preferred orientation; the best apparent gain is ~0.014 Rwp with MD ≈0.95. Ca₃WO₆ texture challenges are now under way, still from the same neutral checkpoint.

Ca₃WO₆ likewise shows only small, diffuse axis gains (Rwp changes under 0.01 so far). This supports keeping its texture neutral, which is especially important because its atomic model is an isovalent structural analogue rather than a directly identified database record.

One Ca₃WO₆ direction, [1 −1 −2], gives a larger but still modest improvement (Rwp 10.136%, MD 0.664). I’ll retain it only if it adds independent family-level improvement after CaWO₄ texture is already present; otherwise it is likely compensating overlap.

The Ca₃WO₆ texture response is highly direction-dependent but small compared with CaWO₄; [1 −1 −2] remains its best isolated branch. I’ll test deletion from the joint model rather than accepting it from this isolated comparison.

The matched triclinic WO₃ source is now undergoing the same 93-axis inventory and replay criteria. This is the decisive fairness check: lower symmetry must beat the monoclinic source after the same nuisance-model opportunities, not merely at the neutral checkpoint.

Several triclinic texture branches are failing the convergence/replay gate despite superficially lower Rwp values; I’m marking those inadmissible. A numerical or bound-collapse gain cannot be used to justify the lower-symmetry source.

The triclinic source is reproducing the same qualitative CaWO₄ texture minima but cannot produce a valid second replay—the saved branch returns a zero-step/non-converged state. I’ll still complete the inventory, then retry the leading branches serially to distinguish a source instability from parallel scheduling.

The leading triclinic branches remain invalid after multiple replays, including [1 −2 −1] and [1 −1 −1]. Their Rwp values are also slightly worse than the corresponding monoclinic branches, so lower symmetry is not currently winning the elaborated comparison.

The triclinic neutral model itself can converge, and its WO₃ texture trials can converge, but adding the strongly supported CaWO₄ orientation repeatedly destabilizes the covariance replay. That source-specific instability is evidence against adopting triclinic WO₃ for the final coupled model.

The triclinic texture sweep is continuing through all sign-distinct axes. Most WO₃ ratios are effectively 1.000, and several branches also fail replay, so no triclinic-specific texture term is emerging to rescue that source.

Even the triclinic analogue of the best CaWO₄ axis is slightly worse than monoclinic (7.988% versus 7.982%) and fails replay. This satisfies the key topology comparison directionally; I’m completing the remaining axes for audit completeness.

No triclinic WO₃ axis has produced a meaningful texture ratio or stable improvement. The repeated zero-step covariance failures are confined to this lower-symmetry coupled basis, strengthening the preference for the replay-stable monoclinic source.

The best numerical triclinic WO₃ texture changes remain under ~0.014 Rwp and are mostly invalid on replay. No diagnostic splitting or position information in the pattern warrants the extra triclinic degrees of freedom.

The remaining triclinic axes continue to refine to MD ≈1.00 with negligible gains. I’m not promoting any of them; the full inventory is being retained only as a source-selection audit.

The lower-symmetry sweep is past the equal-index and into the unequal-index diagonals; none provides a stable physical advantage. The monoclinic source remains the simpler, replay-stable choice.

No sign-distinct diagonal has altered the source decision. I’ll next run the collective low-seed width test for both sources; any branch that binds, turns negative, or cannot survive reseeding will be restored to the neutral checkpoint.

The best late triclinic WO₃ numerical branch (Rwp ≈10.197%) still fails replay and changes MD by only ~2%. It is not retained.

The triclinic WO₃ low-index set is nearly complete; its valid branches are all effectively neutral. This leaves no physical reason to pay for the lower symmetry, especially once the CaWO₄ texture term is included.

All tested triclinic unequal-index WO₃ diagonals remain unsupported. I’m finishing the final axis and then the Ca₃WO₆ set for exact parity with the monoclinic run.

The triclinic-coupled Ca₃WO₆ texture branches are also returning neutral ratios and invalid replays. That confirms the instability is a property of the elaborated lower-symmetry coupled basis, not evidence for a missing texture correction.

The one converged triclinic Ca₃WO₆ branch changes Rwp by only ~0.005 and MD by 1%; it is neutral in practical terms. No additional phase texture is supported.

The triclinic analogue of the isolated Ca₃WO₆ [1 −1 −2] improvement is much smaller and fails replay, another rank instability. This term will not be retained unless it independently survives in the final monoclinic joint model.

No triclinic Ca₃WO₆ axis has survived with a material gain. The complete lower-symmetry axis challenge is almost finished; afterward I’ll move to low-seed collective widths and joint deletion tests on the monoclinic basis.

The last triclinic Ca₃WO₆ unequal-index branches remain at MD≈1 with invalid or negligible changes. This confirms a neutral texture model for WO₃ and Ca₃WO₆.

Both source sweeps are complete. I’m now adding the required anisotropic-size and collective low-seed width branches. Only CaWO₄ isotropic size was physically interior in the phasewise pass; WO₃/Ca₃WO₆ size branches hit the 10 µm neutral boundary and all strain gains required negative values.

CaWO₄ uniaxial size broadening is supported for at least one axis: [010] converges to about 0.199/0.067 µm and lowers Rwp to 9.021%, better than the isotropic-size-only 9.385% branch. I’m completing all axes before combining it with texture.

The uniaxial solutions are all interior; [010] currently leads, with [0 2 −1] and [001] close behind. The broadening direction is therefore not uniquely determined yet, much like the texture axis.

No new axis has beaten [010]. All retained size values remain finite and positive, so this is a genuine family-width effect rather than a boundary artifact.

The monoclinic anisotropy loop selects CaWO₄ [010] as the best isolated size axis (Rwp 9.021%, 0.199/0.067 µm). I’m applying the exact same loop to the triclinic source before any combined model is accepted.

The triclinic [010] size branch is valid and modestly better in isolation (Rwp 8.995% versus 9.021%). The decisive comparison remains the combined texture-plus-width checkpoint, where triclinic texture branches failed replay.

The anisotropic-size ordering is consistent between sources: [010] leads, [0 2 −1]/[001] follow. This family-level consistency supports anisotropic CaWO₄ broadening, while source selection still hinges on replay stability in the elaborated model.

All triclinic anisotropic size branches remain physical and interior, but none changes the qualitative result. I’m waiting for the final few axes, then running collective reseeds for both source bases.

The supported CaWO₄ isotropic size term is replay-stable across a 0.2 µm reseed, converging back to 0.0964 µm and Rwp 9.385%. A fully collective width branch has already destabilized the Ca₃WO₆ metric, so that branch is invalid and will be discarded as required.

The 0.2 and 1.0 µm CaWO₄ reseeds converge to the same 0.0964 µm solution; the very low 0.05 seed falls into a worse non-converged basin. Collective all-phase size branches bind WO₃ at 10 µm and/or collapse Ca₃WO₆, so they are rejected and the checkpoint is restored.

The all-phase 0.05 µm size branch is also worse and non-converged, despite finite nominal sizes. This independently vetoes collective broadening of WO₃/Ca₃WO₆; only the CaWO₄ term survives reseeding.

The monoclinic collective strain and combined branches all require large negative strains (about −7,000 to −15,000) and sometimes collapsed sizes, so every apparent low-Rwp gain is invalid. The triclinic basis is now receiving the same low-seed collective test.

The triclinic collective behavior matches the monoclinic result: the CaWO₄ term reseeds cleanly to ~0.096 µm, while releasing all sizes collapses Ca₃WO₆ and destabilizes its cell. Thus the collective test does not justify extra WO₃/Ca₃WO₆ width terms.

Triclinic reseeds reproduce the same bifurcation: 0.2 and 1.0 µm recover ~0.09594 µm, while 0.05 µm stalls in a worse basin. The supported width term is robust to sensible seeds but not globally unique from an extreme low start; that limitation will be reported.

The triclinic all-size low-seed branch is again worse and non-converged. I’m waiting on the strain branches; based on the phasewise and monoclinic collective tests, any negative-strain minimum will be invalid regardless of nominal Rwp.

As expected, the triclinic collective strain minimum requires large negative strains (roughly −7,800 to −15,700), so its apparent Rwp 8.37% is physically inadmissible. This confirms the same width pruning for both source topologies.

Both source bases now agree: only CaWO₄ broadening is physically supportable; all collective strain/extra-phase width branches bind, collapse, or turn negative. I’m waiting for the final combined branch to terminate, then I’ll construct and delete-test the joint texture/anisotropic-size model.

The neutral-loop gates are now closed. Next I’m building an elaborated checkpoint with the supported CaWO₄ [010] uniaxial size term, then repeating the complete texture-axis inventory for all three phases and both serious WO₃ sources. This tests whether the texture ranking survives broadening rather than trading against it.

At the width-elaborated checkpoint, CaWO₄ [001] texture now gives Rwp 7.339% (valid), substantially better than either correction alone. The full axis set is still required because the neutral texture ranking favored diagonals.

The combined ranking is again diagonal: [1 −2 −1] currently leads at Rwp 6.807%, with all parameters interior. This is a real joint intensity/width improvement, though axis uniqueness still needs the remaining alternatives and deletion tests.

The leading combined axes are tightly grouped: [1 −2 −1] 6.807%, [1 −1 0] 6.819%, [1 −1 −1] 6.880%. I’ll report the direction as model-dependent unless later loops separate them; the existence of CaWO₄ texture itself is robust.

The full CaWO₄ elaborated axis set is complete: [1 −2 −1] leads by only 0.009–0.012 Rwp over [2 −2 −1]/[1 −1 0]. WO₃ axes remain near MD=1 and improve Rwp by only thousandths, so no WO₃ texture is supported.

WO₃’s best elaborated axis changes Rwp by only ~0.008 and MD by ~4%; it is not retained because the response is diffuse and small relative to overlapping phase families. The CaWO₄ term remains the sole supported texture correction.

Ca₃WO₆ texture changes at the width checkpoint are still small (best so far ~0.017 Rwp) and spread across several axes. They do not meet the independent-family support threshold yet.

The strongest Ca₃WO₆ isolated elaborated direction remains [1 −1 −2] (Rwp 8.960%, MD 0.694), but it is still tiny next to the CaWO₄ effect. I’ll test it jointly with CaWO₄ texture and delete it separately.

The remaining Ca₃WO₆ axes do not beat [1 −1 −2]. Its isolated effect is reproducible, but retention still depends on the joint CaWO₄-textured model and coherent residual families rather than Rwp alone.

The monoclinic elaborated loop is complete. I’m now running the identical expanded triclinic loop; if lower symmetry cannot beat and replay the monoclinic 6.807% solution, the monoclinic WO₃ record will be selected.

The triclinic elaborated branches now converge, but the first matched axes are effectively tied with monoclinic (differences below 0.01 Rwp). That is not enough to justify extra cell degrees of freedom without diagnostic splittings.

The decisive [1 −2 −1] branch is essentially identical: monoclinic Rwp 6.807168%, triclinic 6.807320%. Monoclinic is very slightly better and uses fewer cell parameters, so the lower-symmetry source does not win the elaborated checkpoint.

Other diagonal branches differ by only a few thousandths between sources and none beats the monoclinic [1 −2 −1] minimum. The topology choice is therefore stable and favors the simpler monoclinic WO₃ model.

The triclinic CaWO₄ axis inventory is complete; its best is 6.807320%, still not better than monoclinic 6.807168%. I’m completing WO₃/Ca₃WO₆ axes for parity, though their monoclinic responses were already negligible.

Triclinic WO₃ texture changes remain below 0.01 Rwp with ratios close to one. As in the monoclinic loop, no WO₃ orientation term is supported.

No triclinic sign-distinct or unequal-index WO₃ direction has a meaningful effect. The remaining sweep is audit-only and does not threaten the monoclinic selection.

The largest triclinic WO₃ ratio departure so far is only ~5%, with Rwp improvement <0.005. This is insufficient to retain a texture term and does not distinguish the source.

A few triclinic WO₃ directions improve Rwp by ~0.01, but the effects are non-unique and small. They will be pruned; accepting them would overfit overlapping weak reflections.

The triclinic WO₃ texture loop remains physically neutral. I’m continuing through the remaining sign variants to satisfy the complete-axis comparison, but the final topology choice is already stable.

Equal-index triclinic WO₃ diagonals also remain within ~0.01 Rwp of neutral and yield no unique orientation. No term is retained.

The unequal-index positive-sign WO₃ axes are equally negligible. The full sweep is converging cleanly, but clean convergence alone is not evidence for retaining a term.

The late triclinic WO₃ axes remain non-diagnostic; the strongest change is still only ~0.012 Rwp. They are pruned from the final basis.

The complete-axis lower-symmetry comparison remains stable. I’m finishing the last WO₃ and Ca₃WO₆ directions, then will freeze the monoclinic joint checkpoint and run term-deletion plus background/instrument challenges.

The final triclinic WO₃ directions also remain within ~0.01 Rwp and MD≈1. No WO₃ texture term survives.

The full 49-axis triclinic WO₃ inventory is complete with no supported orientation. Ca₃WO₆ is the final parity check; its monoclinic isolated best will later face a joint deletion test.

Triclinic-coupled Ca₃WO₆ [001] changes Rwp by only ~0.012, again small. No phase other than CaWO₄ has yet shown a robust texture effect.

Ca₃WO₆ axis effects are somewhat larger than WO₃ but remain diffuse (several directions at 0.02–0.03 Rwp gain). This still does not establish a unique, family-supported texture correction.

The same Ca₃WO₆ [1 −1 −2] direction is the isolated triclinic best (Rwp 8.932%, MD 0.693), confirming reproducibility. Its survival in the CaWO₄-textured joint model remains the required retention test.

No additional Ca₃WO₆ axis beats [1 −1 −2]. The joint deletion model will determine whether this ~0.06 Rwp isolated gain is independent or merely compensates CaWO₄ intensity mismatch.

The remaining Ca₃WO₆ directions are weaker and do not alter the ranking. The complete source-parity axis loop is nearly closed.

No late Ca₃WO₆ direction approaches [1 −1 −2]. I’ll now finish the last few axes and move directly to the joint model/deletion challenge.

Both elaborated source loops are complete. Monoclinic WO₃ wins the best matched joint branch by 0.00015 Rwp and uses fewer cell variables. I’m now freezing that joint checkpoint, rerunning every CaWO₄ size axis with texture present, and testing isotropic-size deletion so width/texture cannot be trading unnoticed.

Once texture is present, the CaWO₄ uniaxial sizes collapse toward equality (~0.094–0.095 µm). That means anisotropy itself is not independently supported; the likely final width model is a single isotropic CaWO₄ size term.

A key exception has emerged: CaWO₄ uniaxial size along [001] refines to 0.106/0.079 µm and improves Rwp to 6.743%, so anisotropy is independently supported after all. I’m completing the full set before accepting that axis.

The isotropic-size deletion branch returns Rwp 6.8073%, while [001] anisotropy gives 6.7434%; the extra width parameter has a measurable, interior effect. [001] remains the current best axis.

The monoclinic joint size loop actually selects [1 2 0] at Rwp 6.7361%, with [1 2 −1] and [001] within 0.008. I’m applying the same loop to triclinic WO₃; the axis itself remains model-dependent, but anisotropic CaWO₄ broadening is supported.

For matched axes, monoclinic remains as good or slightly better; triclinic [001] gives 6.7459% versus monoclinic 6.7434%. No lower-symmetry advantage is emerging.

The triclinic joint anisotropy ordering matches monoclinic qualitatively, and its matched Rwp values are not lower. The source choice remains monoclinic WO₃.

The matched joint anisotropy loop selects [1 2 0] for both sources; monoclinic remains better (6.7361% versus 6.7393%). Because the accepted width axis changed, I’m reopening the complete texture inventory from that new checkpoint—the required full-loop stability test.

With the accepted [1 2 0] size axis, the first CaWO₄ texture branches remain stable and physical. Their Rwp values move slightly, so I’m waiting for the diagonal minimum to see whether [1 −2 −1] survives.

The selected CaWO₄ texture axis [1 −2 −1] reproduces exactly at Rwp 6.7361% from the new width checkpoint. The coupled width/texture ranking is therefore stabilizing.

The nearest alternative texture axes remain ≥0.08 Rwp worse after the width-axis update. [1 −2 −1] is now a stable texture choice, though the absolute direction is still less certain than the presence of texture.

The CaWO₄ texture ranking has survived the full width update. WO₃ texture is again essentially neutral from the new width-only checkpoint, confirming that its earlier rejection is stable.

No WO₃ direction has produced more than a few thousandths of Rwp improvement at the updated checkpoint. The term remains pruned.

The strongest updated WO₃ texture trial changes MD by ~9% but improves Rwp only ~0.016 and is non-unique; it remains too weak to retain against overlapping families.

The updated complete-axis loop confirms no WO₃ texture. I’m finishing its last directions and then rechecking the Ca₃WO₆ watch axis before freezing the joint model.

Even the best late WO₃ axis improves the width-only checkpoint by only ~0.020 Rwp, with no coherent unique direction. It remains excluded.

Ca₃WO₆ remains weakly texture-sensitive from the updated checkpoint. The decisive [1 −1 −2] watch axis is still pending; it will also be tested jointly with the retained CaWO₄ texture.

Ca₃WO₆ texture gains remain under ~0.012 Rwp before the watch axis, much smaller than CaWO₄ and spread over several directions. No term is retained yet.

The Ca₃WO₆ [1 −1 −2] isolated branch again improves Rwp by ~0.052 with MD 0.707. It is reproducible but still must survive addition to the CaWO₄-textured final basis and separate deletion.

No other Ca₃WO₆ direction rivals [1 −1 −2]. I’m finishing the final axes, then testing the two-texture model directly.

The final parity loop for triclinic WO₃ is running from the accepted [1 2 0] size checkpoint. The first matched axis remains slightly worse than monoclinic; the decisive diagonal is next.

The decisive final branch confirms the source choice: triclinic Rwp 6.7393% versus monoclinic 6.7361%, with two extra cell variables. Lower symmetry does not win.

The neighboring triclinic texture axes are also worse than the monoclinic counterparts. The source ranking survives the full loop.

No alternate CaWO₄ direction reverses the source ranking. I’m completing the audit axes, then the model can move to deletion and instrument/background challenges.

The full CaWO₄ axis set is closed and monoclinic remains preferred. Triclinic WO₃ axes are again near-neutral; these final checks are confirming parity, not altering the model.

No triclinic WO₃ axis has a meaningful effect at the final width checkpoint. The rejection of WO₃ texture is stable.

The strongest triclinic WO₃ direction improves the width-only checkpoint by ~0.022 Rwp, still non-unique and far below the CaWO₄ correction. It remains pruned.

No source-specific WO₃ texture term survives the final loop. The monoclinic source remains preferred on both fit and parsimony.

The remaining equal- and unequal-index triclinic WO₃ axes continue to be weak and non-diagnostic. I’m completing them for audit completeness only.

No late triclinic WO₃ axis changes the conclusion. The final topology and texture basis are stable.

The last triclinic WO₃ directions remain negligible. Once Ca₃WO₆ finishes, no further source-loop iteration is warranted unless a later instrument/background challenge reverses ranking.

The triclinic WO₃ axis set is nearly complete with no supported term. The final source comparison remains unchanged.

All 49 triclinic WO₃ axes are complete with no retained texture. The final Ca₃WO₆ parity set is now running; its watch axis will be compared to the monoclinic result.

Triclinic Ca₃WO₆ texture behavior matches monoclinic qualitatively and remains weak. The watch-axis result is still pending.

No Ca₃WO₆ direction has yet exceeded the small isolated effect seen in the monoclinic run. I’m finishing the last watch branch and remaining axes.

The Ca₃WO₆ watch axis is reproducible in the triclinic basis too (MD≈0.705, ~0.054 Rwp isolated gain), so it is not a WO₃-source artifact. Its final retention now depends solely on the joint two-texture deletion test.

No additional Ca₃WO₆ axis beats [1 −1 −2]. The source-parity loop is almost complete.

The late Ca₃WO₆ directions remain weaker than the watch axis and non-unique. I’m finishing the final few directions now.

The complete source loops are now closed. I’m moving to direct deletion/challenge tests: remove anisotropy, remove texture, add the Ca₃WO₆ watch texture, collectively reopen extra widths/strains, and cross-seed monoclinic/triclinic nuisance parameters.

Deletion confirms both retained CaWO₄ corrections are independent: removing texture raises Rwp to 9.102%; replacing anisotropic size with isotropic raises it to 6.807%. Both branches converge cleanly.

The Ca₃WO₆ watch texture collapses to a negligible joint gain: Rwp 6.7348% versus 6.7361% (≈0.0012), despite its isolated effect. It is compensating overlap and will be pruned.

Reopening WO₃/Ca₃WO₆ sizes from the final checkpoint destabilizes Ca₃WO₆’s metric and fails replay, exactly as in the earlier collective loop. Extra phase-width terms remain rejected.

The apparent collective-strain gain (Rwp 5.71%) is invalid: the replay is zero-step/non-converged and the strain solution violates the nonnegative physical gate. It will not enter the final model.

The triclinic deletion tests mirror monoclinic: removing texture gives Rwp 9.074%, isotropic size 6.807%. The same terms are supported, but triclinic remains the worse, more complex source.

Triclinic Ca₃WO₆ texture also adds only ~0.001 Rwp jointly, confirming it is overlap compensation. It is pruned for both sources.

The triclinic collective extra-size branch fails the same Ca₃WO₆ metric test. This closes the equal collective-width challenge in favor of the pruned model.

The triclinic collective-strain branch also reaches an inadmissible non-converged solution (Rwp 5.75% with invalid strain signs). It is rejected.

Removing Ca₃WO₆ raises Rwp to 9.96%, confirming it is essential and well above a marginal weak-phase effect. Re-adding physical-cell CaO improves Rwp by only ~0.0015; I’ll inspect its scale/families, but such a tiny overlap-only gain cannot satisfy the two-family weak-phase gate.

A major instrument-model signal appeared: refining polarization drops Rwp to 3.31%, far larger than any background change. I will not accept it blindly—I’m checking whether the value stays physical, fractions remain stable, and angular residual trends improve without compensating roughness/background errors.

Roughness can mimic the same large angular-intensity correction (Rwp 3.17–3.55%), so polarization and roughness are strongly competing explanations. I’m treating this as a model-selection problem, not stacking them; the physical parameter values and residual angular trends will decide. Cross-seeding already returns monoclinic to exactly Rwp 6.7361%, confirming its basin is stable.

The nuisance values are physical but competing: polarization refines to 0.858; roughness refines to A=0.378, B=0.548. Fractions shift by less than about 1 wt%. I’m comparing signed residual trends by angle and independent phase-family windows before selecting either correction.

The angular-bin audit favors roughness over polarization: both remove the low/high-angle bias, but roughness A/B gives the lower Rwp (3.166% vs 3.307%) with physical values and similarly stable fractions. I’m now testing direct competition, background order, and the triclinic source under the same roughness model before accepting it.

Under the same roughness model, monoclinic WO₃ now wins decisively: Rwp 3.166% versus triclinic 3.295%. The source ranking strengthens after the accepted intensity correction.

Allowing polarization and roughness together drives roughness A negative (−0.415) and fails the replay gate despite lower nominal Rwp. The combined model is physically invalid; these corrections must remain alternatives, not coexist.

Roughness is selected: it is physical, removes the angular bias, and the polarization+roughness combination fails by driving roughness negative or to a bound. An 8-term background is retained—6 is clearly worse; 10 improves only 0.007 Rwp with two extra oscillatory terms. I’m now repeating the complete source/axis loop under roughness.

Under roughness, alternate CaWO₄ texture axes are already substantially worse (Rwp ≈3.52–3.55%) than the retained 3.166% model. The texture effect survives the intensity correction.

Equatorial and low-index alternatives remain far worse under roughness, so the retained diagonal orientation is not an artifact of the earlier angular-intensity bias.

Roughness changes the texture ranking materially: CaWO₄ [1 −1 0] now gives Rwp 2.779%, beating [1 −2 −1] at 3.166%; [1 −1 −1] is second at 2.935%. I’ll adopt the new axis only after the remaining axes and width loop confirm it.

